{
  "concept": "The plenoptic function L(x, y, θ) records the radiance arriving at every point in a scene from every direction, so a scene's full visual content is a field of directional light distributions — and a pinhole camera is just a device that makes that distribution nearly all zero except along a few rays.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "White scene canvas with a thick black horizontal ground line across the bottom",
    "Large cartoon tree on the right: bright green lobed canopy over a brown forked trunk with two root feet",
    "Small black outlined bird glyph in the upper-right sky",
    "Two free-space sample points in the middle of the scene, drawn as red filled dots (one lower-left, one upper-right)",
    "At each free-space point: a dense radial burst of ~24 black arrows pointing INWARD toward the dot, representing incoming rays from all directions",
    "Pinhole camera on the left: black rectangular box outline (open on the right side where the pinhole sits) with the text label 'Pinhole camera' above it",
    "Pinhole aperture marked as a small gap/point on the right wall of the camera box",
    "Two red sample points on the camera's interior image plane (left wall), upper and lower",
    "Two long crossing arrows from the pinhole to the two interior red points, showing image inversion (upper-right scene ray → lower image point, and vice versa)",
    "Absence of a radial burst at the interior points — only one non-zero direction each",
    "Angular-distribution inset panel: polar plot of intensity L vs direction θ for the currently selected sample point, with a 0–360° angle ring and radius encoding radiance",
    "Direction cursor: a single highlighted ray in the scene plus a matching spoke in the polar plot",
    "Readout text: selected point coordinates (x, y), current direction θ, and value L(x, y, θ)",
    "Legend: red dot = sample point, black inward arrow = incoming light ray, gray = zero radiance"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "movePoint",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "The plenoptic function is defined everywhere — clicking any location in the scene reveals that point's own full angular distribution of incoming light, so the scene is a field of such distributions, not a single image."
    },
    {
      "id": "locationMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Contrasting a free-space point with a point on the camera's image plane: in open air light arrives from all directions (dense distribution), while behind a pinhole nearly every direction is blocked and only one ray survives — which is exactly how a camera turns the plenoptic function into a picture.",
      "label": "Sample location",
      "options": ["Free space", "Inside pinhole camera"],
      "default": "Free space"
    },
    {
      "id": "viewDirection",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Sweeping θ traces one full turn of the plenoptic function at a fixed point: the value spikes when the ray lands on the tree or bird and drops to zero elsewhere, making 'intensity as a function of direction' concrete.",
      "label": "Viewing direction θ (degrees)",
      "range": [0, 360, 1],
      "default": 0
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "A point in open air",
      "narration": "A red dot floating in front of the tree receives light from every direction at once — the dense starburst of inward arrows is one sample of the plenoptic function, L(x, y, θ) for all θ.",
      "state": { "locationMode": "Free space", "viewDirection": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sweep the direction",
      "narration": "Drag θ around the full circle and watch the highlighted ray rotate. The polar plot rises where the ray strikes the green canopy or the bird and falls to near zero toward empty sky — the whole scene is encoded in this one angular profile.",
      "state": { "locationMode": "Free space", "viewDirection": 215 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Move the sample point",
      "narration": "Click somewhere else — higher up, or closer to the tree — and the angular profile changes shape. Every location carries a different slice of the same continuous light field.",
      "state": { "locationMode": "Free space", "viewDirection": 215 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Step inside the pinhole camera",
      "narration": "Now sample a point on the camera's image plane. The starburst collapses: the box walls block all but one direction, so the polar plot is zero everywhere except a single spike through the pinhole.",
      "state": { "locationMode": "Inside pinhole camera", "viewDirection": 30 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Why the image flips",
      "narration": "Follow the two surviving rays: light from the upper scene reaches the lower image point and vice versa. A camera is simply a device that selects one direction of the plenoptic function per pixel — which is precisely the field a NeRF learns to reconstruct.",
      "state": { "locationMode": "Inside pinhole camera", "viewDirection": 200 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "An image is not the light in a scene — it is a razor-thin sample of it: the plenoptic function holds radiance for every point AND every direction, and a pinhole camera works by zeroing out all directions but one per image point.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Wide landscape layout on white, thick black ground line at the bottom. Right third: a big cartoon tree — saturated green lobed canopy, brown forked trunk with two splayed roots — plus a small black outlined bird glyph high in the upper-right corner. Middle third: two red sample dots (one lower-left, one upper-right of the middle area), each surrounded by a dense radial fan of ~24 thin black arrows pointing inward toward the dot, arrowheads near the dot, leaving a small white halo around each red dot. Left third: a black rectangular outline labeled 'Pinhole camera' in a clean sans-serif above it; the right wall is broken by a small pinhole gap; two red dots sit on the left interior wall (upper and lower). Two long straight arrows enter through the pinhole and cross each other, one ending at the upper-left dot and one at the lower-left dot, illustrating inversion. Interactive overlay: a circular polar plot panel (light gray ring, angle ticks at 0/90/180/270) showing radiance vs θ for the selected point, colored warm (orange/red) where non-zero and faded gray where zero; a single highlighted ray in the scene drawn in orange to match the current θ spoke. Readout text in a corner gives x, y, θ, and L. In free-space mode the polar curve is a broad, lumpy closed shape; in camera mode it is flat zero with one thin spike."
}

